.TH CTI-AGENT-SETTINGS 1 "2026-11-22" "cti-agent" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-agent-settings \- split fleet.env into core plus one file per lane
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo cti\-agent\-settings status
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent\-settings split
.BR \-\-dry\-run " | " \-\-for\-real
.SH DESCRIPTION
Step 3 of splitting
.IR fleet.env .
It writes
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.d/00\-core.env
(what several lanes read) and one file per lane (what only that lane reads),
and moves
.I fleet.env
aside. Each unit then loads core and its own file; the check-in loads every
file. The files stay plain text.
.PP
Which file a setting goes in is
.B file:
in
.IR settings.manifest ,
generated from the code. Each setting line is kept as written, with the
comment lines directly above it. A helper other settings use as
.B $NAME
goes before them, or into core when several files use it. A setting no code
reads goes into core and is named.
.PP
It refuses while a secret or login is still in
.I fleet.env
(they are systemd credentials:
.BR cti\-agent\-secrets (1)),
and when a setting would use
.B $NAME
from another lane's file, which its lane never loads. It never prints a
value. Root only.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Split or not; each file and how many settings it holds.
.TP
.B split \-\-dry\-run
Which setting would go to which file, by name.
.TP
.B split \-\-for\-real
Write the files (0640 root and the service group) and move
.I fleet.env
to
.IR fleet.env.pre\-split\-TIME ,
root-only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
done;
.B 1
refused (already split, no fleet.env, a secret still in it, a cross-lane
reference) or the manifest could not be read;
.B 2
bad usage or not root.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.B sudo cti\-agent\-settings split \-\-dry\-run
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent\-settings split \-\-for\-real
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-config check
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fleet\-config (1),
.BR cti\-agent\-secrets (1),
RUNBOOK.md section 11o.
